# Own-wording spells of Deep Magic, chunk k (Spy My Shadow to Touch of the Unliving). TEXT = {name: text}.
TEXT = {
"Spy My Shadow": "Your shadow comes alive as a spy. It can stretch up to ten times your height, move as you direct, and slip under doors, between shutters, and through tiny cracks. It stays two-dimensional and cannot touch physical objects. You see and hear through it as if with your own senses, but magically enhanced senses do not carry through. It can use Stealth normally, with advantage on Dexterity (Stealth) checks in dim light and disadvantage in bright light.",
"St. Blusen's Reaver Spirit": "You fill allies with berserker fury. You and each ally you can see within range gain advantage on Strength checks and Strength saving throws, resistance to bludgeoning, piercing, and slashing damage from nonmagical weapons, and a +2 bonus to damage with melee weapons. When the spell ends, each affected creature must succeed on a Constitution saving throw or gain 1d4 levels of exhaustion.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a spell slot of 3rd level or higher, the melee damage bonus rises by 1 for each slot level above 2nd.",
"St. Parvalas's Risen Road": "You open a glowing portal into the plane of shadow that stays open for 1 minute, until 10 creatures have stepped through, or until you close it (no action required). Those who enter step onto a shadow road in ideal condition that leads to a destination within 50 miles, or in a direction you name. Travelers move at a normal pace and cannot rest. If anyone stops for more than 10 minutes, the spell ends and drops everyone at a random spot within 10 miles of the start. The road lasts 2d6 hours. When it ends, travelers are set down safely near the destination, or 50 miles from the start in the chosen direction. No one gains exhaustion from travel on the road. The road then vanishes along with anything left on it. Each casting makes a new road. Whether it crosses an existing shadow road, allowing meetings with other travelers or monsters or a change of destination, is up to the GM.",
"St. Whiteskull's Borrowing": "You touch a creature and borrow one of its senses, movement types and speeds, feats, languages, immunities, or extraordinary abilities for the duration. The target keeps the use of the ability. An unwilling target avoids the effect with a successful Constitution saving throw. The target can be alive or dead for no longer than 1 minute. A corpse makes no saving throw. You can hold only one borrowed power at a time.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a 5th-level slot, the duration becomes 1 hour and the target loses the borrowed power while the spell lasts.",
"Staff of Violet Fire": "You conjure a quarterstaff of pure necrotic energy that blazes with violet light in your chosen hand. If you let go, it vanishes, but you can call it back as a bonus action while you keep concentrating. It is an unstable, temporary magic item with 10 charges and needs no attunement. The wielder can use it in one of two ways. Spend 1 charge when you make a melee attack with it, and a hit deals 5d10 necrotic damage. Spend 2 charges to loose necrotic bolts as ranged attacks against up to 3 targets, each dealing 1d8 + 4 necrotic damage. The staff disappears and the spell ends when all charges are spent or you stop concentrating.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 6th level or higher, the melee damage rises by 1d10 for every two slot levels above 4th, or you add one more ranged bolt for every two slot levels above 4th.",
"Stanch": "The target's blood clots at once. A dying target becomes stable, and any ongoing bleeding or wounding effect on it ends. The target cannot serve as the blood source for any spell or effect that needs even a drop of blood.",
"Star's Heart": "Gravity in a 50-foot radius centered on you becomes ten times stronger. Every other creature in the area drops what it holds, falls prone, becomes incapacitated, and cannot move. A flying or levitating creature that lands on something solid takes three times the normal falling damage. Any creature other than you that enters the area or starts its turn there must succeed on a Strength saving throw or fall prone and become incapacitated and unable to move. A creature that starts its turn prone and incapacitated makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure it takes 8d6 bludgeoning damage. On a success it takes half as much, is no longer incapacitated, and can move at half speed. Ranged weapon attacks inside the area have a normal range of 5 feet and a maximum range of 10 feet. This also applies to spells that create missiles with mass, such as flaming sphere. A creature under freedom of movement or similar magic has advantage on the Strength saves and keeps its full speed after it recovers from being incapacitated.",
"Starburst": "A mote of starlight appears in a 5-foot cube you can see within range and explodes. A creature in the cube must succeed on a Charisma saving throw or take 1d8 radiant damage. The damage rises to 2d8 at 5th level, 3d8 at 11th level, and 4d8 at 17th level.",
"Starfall": "Bolts of starlight fall from the sky onto up to five creatures you can see within range. Each bolt hits one target, deals 6d6 radiant damage, knocks it prone, and blinds it until the start of your next turn. A successful Dexterity saving throw halves the damage and avoids the prone and blinded effects. If you name fewer than five targets, the extra bolts hit the ground and do nothing.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 6th level or higher, you create one more bolt for each slot level above 5th.",
"Starry Vision": "This spell works like compelling fate with these changes. You cast it as a reaction when an enemy starts its turn, it has twice the range, and it lasts up to 1 minute. At the end of each of its turns, the target repeats the Charisma saving throw and ends the effect on a success.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 8th level or higher, the bonus to AC rises by 1 for each slot level above 7th.",
"Steal Warmth": "You cast this as a reaction when magic deals cold damage to you. Choose a living creature within 5 feet of you. That creature takes the cold damage instead, or half of it with a successful Constitution saving throw. You regain hit points equal to the cold damage the target takes.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 4th level or higher, the distance to the target increases by 5 feet for each slot level above 3rd.",
"Steam Blast": "Superheated steam bursts out in a 15-foot radius around you. Every other creature in the area takes 5d8 fire damage, or half as much with a successful Dexterity saving throw. Nonmagical fires smaller than a bonfire go out, and everything in the area gets wet.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 5th level or higher, the damage rises by 1d8 for each slot level above 4th.",
"Steam Whistle": "You open your mouth and let out a shattering scream. Every other creature in a 30-foot radius around you takes 10d10 thunder damage and is deafened for 1d8 hours. A successful Constitution saving throw halves the damage and shortens the deafness to 1 round.",
"Stench of Rot": "Choose a creature you can see within range that is not a construct or undead. It must succeed on a Charisma saving throw or be cursed for the duration. A cursed target reeks of death and rot, and only magic can hide or remove the smell. It has disadvantage on all Charisma checks and on Constitution saving throws to keep concentration on spells. A creature with Keen Smell or a similar trait that shows a strong sense of smell adds your spellcasting ability modifier to Wisdom (Perception) and Wisdom (Survival) checks to find the target. Remove curse or similar magic ends the spell early.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 3rd level or higher, the duration increases by 1 hour for each slot level above 2nd.",
"Step Like Me": "Choose a creature you can see within range that is within one size category of you. It must succeed on a Constitution saving throw or you steal its footsteps. For the duration, you leave the target's tracks and it leaves tracks as if it were you. Creatures that identify others by tremorsense also mistake you for a creature of the target's kind.",
"Sting of the Scorpion Goddess": "You grow a large scorpion's tail with a venomous stinger. For the duration, you can use a bonus action to make a melee spell attack with the stinger against a creature within 5 feet. On a hit, you deal 1d6 piercing damage and 2d8 poison damage, and the target is poisoned for 1 minute. A creature hit by the stinger makes a Constitution saving throw. On a success it takes half the poison damage and is not poisoned.",
"Storm Form": "You turn into a living storm cloud, a swirl of black clouds lit by flickers of lightning. Your only movement is a flying speed of 60 feet, and you can enter other creatures' spaces. You gain resistance to nonmagical damage, immunity to lightning damage, and advantage on Strength, Dexterity, and Constitution saving throws. A creature that hits you with a melee weapon attack takes 3d8 lightning damage. You can pass through small holes and cracks, you treat liquids as solid ground, and you cannot fall. You hover even if stunned or incapacitated. You cannot speak, handle objects, or cast spells, and anything you carry cannot be used, dropped, or touched. You have three actions. Attack a creature up to 30 feet away, dealing 3d8 lightning damage on a hit. Bring rain down on a 5-foot square within reach, soaking it and putting out nonmagical fires there. Or expand into a 20-foot-radius burst of rain, wind, lightning, and thunder. Each creature in it is soaked, takes 3d8 lightning and 3d8 thunder damage, is deafened for 1d4 rounds, and is knocked prone. A successful Constitution saving throw halves the damage and avoids the deafened and prone conditions. The burst uses up the rest of the duration, and you return to normal at the end of your turn.",
"Storm God's Doom": "A strong wind swirls from your hand to a point you choose within range and bursts into a vortex. Each creature in a 20-foot-radius cylinder centered on that point makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, a creature takes 3d8 bludgeoning damage, is pulled to the center, and is thrown 50 feet into the air. If it hits a solid obstruction such as a ceiling, it takes bludgeoning damage as if it fell 50 feet minus the distance it rose. For example, hitting a ceiling after rising 10 feet deals the damage of a 40-foot fall, which is 4d6. The creature then falls and takes the remaining fall damage as usual.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 4th level or higher, the distance creatures are thrown rises by 10 feet for each slot level above 3rd. In effect, this adds 1d6 bludgeoning damage per level above 3rd.",
"Storm of Wings": "You create a 15-foot-radius sphere of spectral birds, bats, or insects centered on a point you can see within range. The storm spreads around corners and its area is lightly obscured. A creature in the storm when it appears, and each creature that starts its turn there, is affected. As a bonus action you can move the storm up to 30 feet. As an action you can switch it to another type. Bats: 4d6 necrotic damage, and the creature's speed is halved while in the storm. Birds: 4d6 slashing damage, and the creature has disadvantage on attack rolls while in the storm. Insects: 4d6 poison damage, and the creature makes a Constitution saving throw each time it casts a spell while in the storm. On a failure the spell fails and the action is lost, but the spell slot is not spent.",
"Strength of an Ox": "You touch a creature. For the duration it can carry, push, drag, and lift as if it were one size category larger. It can also carry loads that would normally be unwieldy, such as a large log, a rowboat, or an oxcart.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 2nd level or higher, you affect one more creature for each slot level above 1st.",
"Strength of the Underworld": "You call on the dark gods of the afterlife to strengthen the undead energy in a touched target. For the duration it has advantage on saving throws against Turn Undead. If you cast this on a corpse that died of darakhul fever, the corpse gains a +5 bonus on the roll to see whether it rises as a Darakhul Ghoul.",
"Sudden Dawn": "You call morning to arrive early. With a sharp word you make a 30-foot-radius cylinder of light centered on a point on the ground within range. It rises 100 feet or until it meets an obstruction such as a ceiling. The area inside is brightly lit.",
"Sudden Stampede": "You conjure fey spirits shaped like galloping horses. They run in a line 10 feet wide and 60 feet long, in a direction you choose from a point within range, trampling everything in their path before vanishing. Each creature in the line takes 6d10 bludgeoning damage and is knocked prone. A successful Dexterity saving throw halves the damage and avoids being knocked prone.",
"Summon Avatar": "You summon a worldly incarnation of a Great Old One in an unoccupied space you can see within range. Choose the type. Avatar of Cthulhu: a deep one that can cast command and sleep of the deep at will. Avatar of Nyarlathotep: a dark voice that can cast bane and semblance of dread at will. Avatar of Shub-Niggurath: a goat-man that can cast black goat's blessing and unseen strangler at will. Avatar of Yog-Sothoth: a human with 1d4 + 1 flesh warps that can cast gift of resilience and thunderwave at will. The GM may allow other options. When the avatar appears, you make a Charisma saving throw. On a success it is friendly to you and your allies. On a failure it is friendly to no one and attacks the nearest creature, pursuing it as long as it can. The avatar rolls initiative and takes its own turns. If friendly, it obeys your verbal commands (no action required), and with no command it attacks the nearest creature. Each round you keep concentrating, you must succeed on a DC 15 Wisdom saving throw at the end of your turn or take 1d6 psychic damage. If your total damage from this exceeds your Wisdom score, you gain 1 point of Void taint and suffer short-term madness. The same penalty applies again when the total passes twice your Wisdom score, three times, and so on. The avatar vanishes when it drops to 0 hit points or the spell ends. If you stop concentrating before 1 hour passes, the avatar turns uncontrolled and hostile until it vanishes 1d6 rounds later or is killed.",
"Summon Eldritch Servitor": "You summon eldritch aberrations in unoccupied spaces you can see within range. Choose either two ghasts of Leng or one shantak. When they appear, you make a Charisma saving throw. On a success they are friendly to you and your allies. On a failure they are friendly to no one and attack the nearest creatures, pursuing them as long as they can. They roll initiative and take their turns as a group. If friendly, they obey your verbal commands (no action required), and with no command they attack the nearest living creature. Each round you keep concentrating, you must succeed on a DC 15 Wisdom saving throw at the end of your turn or take 1d4 psychic damage. If your total damage from this exceeds your Wisdom score, you gain 1 point of Void taint and suffer short-term madness. The same penalty applies again when the total passes twice your Wisdom score, three times, and so on. A summoned creature vanishes when it drops to 0 hit points or the spell ends. If you stop concentrating before 1 hour passes, they turn uncontrolled and hostile until they vanish 1d6 rounds later or are killed.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a 7th- or 8th-level slot, you can summon four ghasts of Leng or one hound of Tindalos. Using a 9th-level slot, you can summon five ghasts of Leng or one nightgaunt.",
"Summon Old One's Avatar": "You summon a worldly incarnation of a Great Old One in an unoccupied space you can see within range. It appears as a Void speaker strengthened by gifts from the Void. Choose the type. Avatar of Cthulhu: the Void speaker is a deep one that can cast command and sleep of the deep at will. Avatar of Nyarlathotep: a dark voice that can cast bane and semblance of dread at will. Avatar of Shub-Niggurath: a goat-man that can cast black goat's blessing and unseen strangler at will. Avatar of Yog-Sothoth: a commoner with 1d4 + 1 more flesh warps that can cast gift of Azathoth and thunderwave at will. The GM may allow other options. When the avatar appears, you make a Charisma saving throw. On a success it is friendly to you and your allies. On a failure it is friendly to no one and attacks the nearest creature, pursuing it as long as it can. The avatar rolls initiative and takes its own turn. If friendly, it obeys your verbal commands (no action required), and with no command it attacks the nearest creature. Each round you keep concentrating, you must succeed on a DC 15 Wisdom saving throw or take 1d6 psychic damage. If your cumulative damage from this exceeds your Wisdom score, you gain 1 point of Void taint and suffer short-term madness. The same penalty applies again when the total passes twice your Wisdom score, three times, and so on. The avatar vanishes when it drops to 0 hit points or the spell ends. If you stop concentrating before 1 hour passes, it turns uncontrolled and hostile and stays until 1d6 rounds later or until it is killed.",
"Summon Star": "You call a friendly star down from the heavens. It appears in an unoccupied space you can see within range as a glowing humanoid with long white hair. Every creature other than you that sees the star must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or be charmed for the duration. A charmed creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns. On a success the charm ends and the creature is immune to this casting. In every other way the star is a deva. It understands your verbal commands and obeys them (no action required). With no command, it defends itself and attacks the last creature that attacked it. The star vanishes when it drops to 0 hit points or the spell ends.",
"Sun's Bounty": "Sunlight heals and feeds you or a creature you touch. For the duration, the target regains 1 hit point at the start of each of its turns while bathed in direct sunlight. 30 minutes or more of exposure also provides a day's normal nourishment. In dim light the healing drops to 1 hit point per minute. Normal or magical darkness suppresses the spell. While in sunlight, the target can ignore one level of exhaustion, and the level is only set aside for a time. Undead are especially vulnerable. A targeted undead that fails its Wisdom saving throw becomes poisoned and takes radiant damage instead of healing while in sunlight.",
"Surge Dampener": "You touch a creature other than yourself and anchor it firmly in reality, shielding it from chaos magic surges. The protected creature can make a DC 13 Charisma saving throw to negate a chaos magic surge effect that normally allows no save, or it has advantage on a save the surge normally allows. Once the protected creature succeeds on a save that this spell affected, the spell ends.",
"Surprise Blessing": "You touch a willing creature and choose one condition it currently has. The condition's normal effect is suspended and the effect below applies instead, lasting as long as the original condition or until the spell ends. If the spell ends before the condition would, you gain the condition for the rest of its duration, even if you were not the original recipient. Blinded: truesight to 10 feet, and it can see 10 feet into the Ethereal Plane. Charmed: its Charisma score becomes 19 unless already higher, and it is immune to charm effects. Frightened: it emits a 10-foot-radius aura of dread. Each creature it chooses that starts its turn in the aura must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or be frightened of it. A creature frightened this way that starts its turn outside the aura repeats the save and ends the condition on a success. Paralyzed: it can use one extra bonus action or reaction per round. Petrified: it gains a +2 bonus to AC. Poisoned: it heals 2d6 hit points at the start of its next turn and becomes immune to poison damage and the poisoned condition. Stunned: it has advantage on Intelligence, Wisdom, and Charisma saving throws.",
"Swift Exchange": "When you or another creature you can see within range is targeted by a weapon attack or spell attack, you can instantly swap places with that creature. An unwilling creature can make a Wisdom saving throw to avoid the swap. Otherwise you both appear in the other's former space in an instant. The attack resolves against whichever of you now stands in the targeted space.",
"Symbol of Sorcery": "You draw an arcane symbol on an object, wall, or other surface at least 5 feet wide. When a creature other than you comes within 5 feet of it, the symbol explodes. Each creature in a 60-foot cone must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or be stunned. A stunned creature repeats the save at the end of each of its turns and ends the effect on a success. After the symbol explodes or the duration runs out, its power is spent and the spell ends.",
"Talons of a Hungry Land": "Three parallel lines of flared obsidian spikes burst from the ground on a solid surface within range. They last for the duration and give three-quarters cover to creatures behind them. You can make lines up to 60 feet long, 10 feet high, and 5 feet thick, or form a circle 20 feet across, up to 15 feet high and 5 feet thick. When the lines appear, each creature in their area makes a Dexterity saving throw, taking 8d8 slashing damage on a failure or half as much on a success. A creature can move through the lines but cuts itself on the edges. Each 1 foot moved through costs 4 feet of movement. The first time a creature enters the lines on a turn, or ends its turn there, it makes a Dexterity saving throw, taking 8d8 slashing damage on a failure or half as much on a success. When you stop concentrating, you can make the spikes explode. Each creature within 15 feet takes 5d8 slashing damage, or half as much with a successful Dexterity saving throw.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 8th level or higher, the damage from all effects of the lines rises by 1d8 for each slot level above 7th.",
"Targeting Foreknowledge": "Twisting the blade, striking with the butt of a spear, and slashing again as you recover from a lunge are all ways to get more from your weapon. Cast this as a bonus action right after you hit with a melee weapon attack. The target takes an extra 2d6 damage of the weapon's type. If your attack roll was a 19 or higher, treat this extra attack as a critical hit and double the damage dice.",
"Telekinetic Parry": "As a reaction, you use a quick burst of kinetic force to block an attack against you. The attacker has disadvantage on the attack roll. If the attack still hits, it deals damage as normal. This works on melee attacks, ranged attacks, and spell attacks with a physical form, including force effects, but not on spells that need no attack roll.",
"Telekinetic Trip": "You lash out with telekinesis and sweep a creature within range off its feet. The target must succeed on a Dexterity saving throw or fall prone.",
"Terrifying Lash": "A faintly glowing whip of energy forms in your hand. It has 10 feet of reach. While you concentrate, you can use your action to make a melee attack roll against a target, adding your spellcasting ability modifier to the roll. On a hit, the target takes 2d10 psychic damage and makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a failure it is frightened for 1d4 rounds. If the target is already frightened, you have advantage on the attack roll and it has disadvantage on the Wisdom save.",
"Thin the Ice": "Choose a point within range. It becomes the top center of a cylinder 10 feet in radius and 40 feet deep, and all ice inside melts at once. The top layer of ice looks sturdy but covers a 40-foot-deep pit of ice water. A successful Wisdom (Survival) check or passive Perception check against your spell save DC spots the thin ice. When a creature weighing more than 20 pounds (or a larger weight you set when casting) walks onto the cylinder or is already standing on it, the ice gives way. Unless it succeeds on a Dexterity saving throw, it falls into the icy water and takes 2d6 cold damage, plus whatever other trouble water, armor, or a freezing climate causes. The water slowly refreezes on its own.",
"Thorn Cage": "Thick, thorny vines burst from the ground around a target you choose. The target must succeed on a Strength saving throw or be restrained by the vines until the spell ends. A restrained creature can use its action to make a Strength check against your spell save DC. Each attempt deals 2d6 piercing damage to it from the thorns, and on a success it breaks free.",
"Thousand Darts": "You fire thousands of needlelike darts in a line 5 feet wide and 120 feet long. Each creature in the line takes 6d6 piercing damage, or half as much with a successful Dexterity saving throw. The first creature the darts strike makes its save with disadvantage.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 4th level or higher, the damage rises by 1d6 for each slot level above 3rd.",
"Throes of Ecstasy": "Choose a humanoid you can see within range. It must succeed on a Constitution saving throw or be overcome with euphoria and become incapacitated for the duration. It automatically fails Wisdom saving throws, and attack rolls against it have advantage. At the end of each of its turns, the target can repeat the Constitution save. On a success the spell ends on it and it gains one level of exhaustion. If the spell runs its full duration, the target gains three levels of exhaustion when it ends.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 4th level or higher, you can target one more humanoid for each slot level above 3rd. The humanoids must be within 30 feet of each other.",
"Thunder Bolt": "You hurl a knot of thunder at an enemy. Make a ranged spell attack. On a hit, the target takes 1d8 thunder damage and cannot take reactions until the start of your next turn. The damage rises to 2d8 at 5th level, 3d8 at 11th level, and 4d8 at 17th level.",
"Thunderclap": "You clap your hands and release a peal of thunder. Each creature within 20 feet of you takes 8d4 thunder damage and is deafened for 1d8 rounds. A successful Constitution saving throw halves the damage and avoids the deafness. A creature that fails the save by 5 or more is also stunned for 1 round. The spell does not work in an area under silence. Very brittle material such as crystal may shatter if within range, at the GM's discretion. A character holding such an object can protect it with a successful Dexterity saving throw.",
"Thunderous Charge": "With a thunderous battle cry, you move up to 10 feet in a straight line and make a melee weapon attack. On a hit, you choose one: gain a +5 bonus to the attack's damage, or shove the target 10 feet.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 2nd level or higher, the distance you can move rises by 10 feet and the attack deals 1d6 extra thunder damage for each slot level above 1st.",
"Thunderous Stampede": "This spell works like thunderous charge, but affects up to three targets within range, including you. A target other than you must use its reaction to move and attack under this spell.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 3rd level or higher, the distance targets can move rises by 10 feet and the attack deals 1d6 extra thunder damage for each slot level above 2nd.",
"Thunderous Wave": "You set off a shock wave at a point you choose within range. It bursts outward in a 30-foot-radius sphere. The wave deals no damage by itself, but each creature it passes through makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure, the creature is pushed 30 feet and knocked prone, and if it hits a solid obstruction it takes 5d6 bludgeoning damage. On a success, the creature is pushed 15 feet and not knocked prone, and an obstruction takes half as much damage. The spell also makes a boom that can be heard within 400 feet.",
"Thunderstorm": "You touch a willing creature and wrap it in a roiling storm cloud 30 feet in diameter that crackles with harmless thunder and lightning. The creature gains a flying speed of 60 feet. The cloud heavily obscures the creature from view, though the creature itself can see through it.",
"Tick Stop": "You speak a word, and the target construct can take either an action or a bonus action on its next turn, not both. The construct is immune to further tick stops from you for 24 hours.",
"Tidal Barrier": "A swirling wave of seawater crashes and rolls in a 10-foot radius around your space. The area is difficult terrain. A creature that starts its turn there, or enters it for the first time on a turn, makes a Strength saving throw. On a failure it is pushed 10 feet away from you and its speed becomes 0 until the start of its next turn.",
"Time Jaunt": "You step outside the normal flow of time and seem to vanish for 1 round. You can quietly alter the scene, appearing for an instant each time you help an ally or hinder a foe. To everyone else you disappear when you cast the spell, flicker in and out of view during the next round, and then reappear for good in the space you left, which ends the spell. You cannot be attacked by any means while the spell lasts. You can take no other actions during the round. As the round plays out, you can give each creature involved in the combat one of these effects. Give it advantage or disadvantage on a saving throw it is about to make. Give it advantage or disadvantage on an attack roll it is about to make. Move a mobile object to give or remove cover for a creature. Shift objects into a creature's path so the first 10 feet of terrain it must cross become difficult terrain. After an attack roll is found to hit, adjust the angle of the strike or the target's position, so the strike deals half damage or gains a damage bonus equal to half your level.\n\n**Example.** A party fights four trolls. The cleric casts time jaunt, then during the round turns a troll to give the rogue advantage, nudges the fighter's shield to give a troll disadvantage on its attack, flips a table to give the rogue cover, spills a barrel to make a troll's path difficult terrain, makes one troll's hit glance for half damage, kicks two trolls behind the knee so they have disadvantage on Dexterity saves and fall prone, and shifts a troll's arm to give the fighter a damage bonus of +7, half the cleric's level. The cleric then reappears and takes the next turn in round 2 as normal.",
"Time Jump": "You touch a construct and hurl it forward in time unless it succeeds on a Constitution saving throw. The construct vanishes for 1d4 + 1 rounds. During that time it cannot act and nothing can affect it. When it returns, it is unaware that any time passed.",
"Time Loop": "You catch a creature within range in a loop of time. The target is teleported to the space where it began its most recent turn, then makes a Wisdom saving throw. On a success the spell ends. On a failure the creature must repeat what it did on its previous turn, following the same sequence of moves and actions as best it can. It need not follow the same path or attack the same target, but if it moved and then attacked last turn, its only option is to move and then attack. If the space where it began its previous turn is occupied, or it cannot take the same action (for example, it cast a spell it can no longer cast), the target becomes incapacitated. An affected target repeats the saving throw at the end of each of its turns and ends the effect on itself on a success. While the spell lasts, the target teleports back to its starting point at the start of each of its turns and must repeat the same sequence.",
"Time Slippage": "You trap a creature within range so that different parts of its body run at different speeds. The creature makes an Intelligence saving throw. On a failure it is stunned until the end of its next turn. On a success its speed is halved and it has disadvantage on attack rolls and saving throws until the end of its next turn. It repeats the save at the end of each of its turns, with the same results. For the duration, the chronal distortion in its body also gives it disadvantage on Strength and Dexterity saving throws and advantage on Constitution and Charisma saving throws. The spell ends if the creature succeeds on three saves in a row.",
"Time Step": "You step briefly forward in time. You vanish from your location and reappear at the start of your next turn in a spot you can see within 30 feet of the space you left. Nothing that happens while you are gone can affect you, and you are not aware of it.",
"Time Vortex": "You destabilize time and create a vortex of temporal ripples, visible as a spherical distortion with a 10-foot radius centered on a point within range. Each creature in the area when you cast the spell must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or be affected. A creature that enters the sphere or starts its turn inside must also make the save. On a success it becomes immune to this casting. An affected creature cannot take reactions and rolls a d10 at the start of its turn to see how it acts that turn. On a 1 or 2, it is affected as if by slow until the start of its next turn. On a 3 to 5, it is stunned until the start of its next turn. On a 6 to 8, its current initiative result drops by 5, and it uses the new result from the next round on, with repeated results stacking. On a 9 or 10, its speed is halved (rounded up to the nearest 5 feet) until the start of its next turn. You can move the vortex 10 feet each round as a bonus action. An affected creature can repeat the saving throw at the end of each of its turns and ends the effect on itself on a success.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 5th level or higher, the sphere's radius rises by 5 feet for each slot level above 4th.",
"Time in a Bottle": "You choose a spot within your sight. Time within a 20-foot radius of it comes under your control. While you keep concentrating, you can freeze it, reverse it, or advance it by up to 1 minute. Nothing and no one, you included, can enter the field or affect what happens inside it. You can end the effect at any moment as an action on your turn.",
"Timeless Engine": "You halt the normal degrading and wear of a nonmagical clockwork device, so it needs no regular maintenance and burns fuel at 1/10 the normal rate. For magical devices and constructs, the spell greatly reduces wear. A magical clockwork device, machine, or creature that normally needs daily maintenance now needs care only once a year, and one that needed monthly maintenance now needs attention only once a decade.",
"Timely Distraction": "You raise a swirling, crackling wave of pops, flashes, and flying debris that can confound one creature. If the target fails a Wisdom saving throw, roll a d4. On a 1 it is blinded, on a 2 stunned, on a 3 deafened, and on a 4 knocked prone. An affected creature can repeat the save at the end of each of its turns and ends the effect on itself on a success. Otherwise the spell ends when its duration runs out.",
"Tireless": "You give a touched creature machine-like stamina for the duration. It needs no food, drink, or rest. During overland travel it moves at three times its normal speed and can do three times the usual amount of labor. It gains no protection from fatigue or exhaustion caused by a magical effect.",
"Tolling Doom": "A deep bell tolls from somewhere above and beyond, judging your enemies and foretelling their doom. Up to eight creatures you can see within range make Charisma saving throws. Whenever a target that failed the save makes an attack roll or saving throw before the spell ends, it must roll a d4 and subtract the result. You have advantage on any attack against a target that failed its save. The spell has no effect on a target with an Intelligence score lower than 3.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 7th level or higher, you can affect one more target for each slot level above 6th.",
"Tome Curse": "You protect a book by writing a curse in it with ink made from the spell's material components. The text is usually a couplet or quatrain warning against disturbing your property. You set the trigger, such as reading the book without permission, damaging it, or stealing it. When the curse triggers, the creature that did it must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or suffer the effect. You can choose an effect from bestow curse or one of these. The target is blinded while cursed. Beasts are unusually hostile to the target while it is cursed and have advantage on attack rolls against it. The target becomes an attractive mark for pickpockets and thieves, who try to steal its valuables when they can. The cursed book itself does not count as one of the target's valuables, even if the target holds it. When the cursed target receives magical healing or rolls Hit Dice to regain hit points after a long rest, it rolls twice and uses the lower result. Remove curse ends this effect. You can also set other actions that would end the curse, such as returning a stolen book.",
"Tongue Tied": "You make a choking gesture at a target, which must succeed on a Wisdom saving throw or be unable to speak clearly. Its speech turns to garble and it has disadvantage on Charisma checks that need speech. It can cast a spell with a verbal component only by succeeding on a Constitution check against your spell save DC. On a failed check its action is used but the spell slot is not spent.",
"Tongue of Sand": "This spell is much like magic mouth. You plant a message of up to 25 words in sand that fills a space of at least 4 square feet and is at least 2 inches deep. You also set the conditions that trigger the message. When triggered, a mouth forms in the sand and speaks the message in a raspy whisper that creatures within 10 feet can hear. The sand can also answer briefly. For up to 10 minutes after the trigger, questions put to the sand are answered as you would answer them. Each answer can be no more than ten words, and the spell ends after the second answer.",
"Torrent of Fire": "You draw on fire held in ley lines and unleash a 60-foot cone of flame. Creatures in the cone take 6d6 fire damage, or half as much with a successful Dexterity saving throw. You can then flow along the flames and reappear anywhere inside the cone. This repositioning does not count as movement and does not provoke opportunity attacks.",
"Touch of the Unliving": "Make a melee spell attack against a creature within your reach. On a hit, the target takes 2d6 necrotic damage, and if it is not undead it is paralyzed until the end of its next turn. Until the spell ends, you can make the attack again on each of your turns as an action.\n\n**At Higher Levels.** Using a slot of 4th level or higher, the damage rises by 1d6 for each slot level above 3rd.",
}
